      "hints": [
        "Differentiate the composite function $(\\tan\\theta)^2$ using the chain rule.",
        "The outer square contributes $2\\tan\\theta$ and $\\frac{d}{d\\theta}(\\tan\\theta)=\\sec^2\\theta$.",
        "Use $\\sec^2\\theta=1/\\cos^2\\theta$ to put the derivative in the displayed form."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Let $u = \\tan\\theta$, so that $\\tan^2\\theta = u^2$.",
        "Apply the chain rule $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}(u^2) = 2u\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}$:\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}(\\tan\\theta) = \\sec^2\\theta$$\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}(\\tan^2\\theta) = 2\\tan\\theta \\sec^2\\theta$$",
        "Use the reciprocal trigonometric identity $\\sec^2\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos^2\\theta}$:\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}(\\tan^2\\theta) = 2\\tan\\theta \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\cos^2\\theta}\\right) = \\dfrac{2\\tan\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta}$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Differentiation": "The chain rule gives $\\frac d{d\\theta}(\\tan^2\\theta)=2\\tan\\theta\\sec^2\\theta=\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta}$."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
      }
